    not_duplicate_of: []  # 1e5f
    pr_ref:  # 1e5f
    description: "## Statement\n\nExtend the WI-palol promote phase in `refine_frameworks` to handle bare-name framework patterns (`django`, `flask`, `rails`, `tokio`, etc.) via a compound-import-required gate: a bare-name pattern `P` promotes when a prod-non-test source file imports a module matching `P` via PREFIX (e.g., `django.db` matches pattern `django` via the `.db` suffix), NOT via exact match alone.\n\nThis closes cohorts 2+3 of INV-rojip's closure spec (bakerydemo Django, chatwoot Rails with Gemfile-declared Rails — the bare-name surface where WI-himas's `-r` chain follower and WI-lohok's autoload exemption don't apply because the manifest is genuinely silent).\n\n## Root cause continuity\n\nWI-palol shipped the structural promote phase but gated it on `_is_specific_pattern` (scoped, slash-compound, or dot-compound). Bare names like `django`, `flask`, `rails` were intentionally deferred per the WI-rofiz lesson: bare `graphql` was a false-positive trigger because non-GraphQL repos installed it for type definitions or codegen, doing `import graphql`-style exact imports that misclassified the repo.\n\nThe empirical pattern observed across real repos:\n\n- **Build-tooling false positives** (the WI-rofiz failure mode) are almost always EXACT bare imports: `import graphql` for typedefs, `import react` for tree-shaking metadata, `import flask` in a one-off CLI script.\n- **Real framework usage** is almost always COMPOUND-submodule imports: `from django.db import models`, `from django.urls import path`, `from flask.app import Flask`, `require 'rails/generators'`, `use tokio::sync::Mutex`.\n\n`_module_matches` already distinguishes these two arms — exact equality vs. prefix-with-separator — but the promote phase doesn't consult which arm fired. The compound-import-required gate fixes that by requiring the prefix arm to fire.\n\n## Prior occurrences (continued from INV-rojip)\n\n| WI | Surface | Fix shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| WI-lohok | Rails autoload with Gemfile declaration | `_AUTOLOAD_BY_CONVENTION_FRAMEWORKS` skip-demote |\n| WI-himas | Python layered `requirements/` + `-r` chains | Recursive manifest scanner |\n| WI-rofiz | Bare `graphql` npm FP from typedef installs | Drop bare-name from dependency-name list |\n| WI-donud | Apollo workspace-scoped import | YAML relocation (graphql.yaml → node-http.yaml) |\n| WI-palol | Workspace scoped/compound import promotion | Promote phase with specificity gate (scoped/`/`/`.`) |\n| **WI-bare-promote** (this WI) | Bare-name framework with compound-submodule prod imports | Promote phase extended with compound-import arm |\n\n## Proposed mechanism\n\nExtend `_has_prod_import_match` (or add a sibling helper) to return not just \"did any pattern match any prod-non-test importer\" but also \"did the match fire via the prefix arm (compound) or the exact arm (bare exact).\" Then the promote phase becomes:\n\n1. **Specific-pattern path (existing WI-palol behavior):** patterns passing `_is_specific_pattern` promote on any prod-non-test importer regardless of match arm.\n2. **NEW bare-pattern path:** patterns NOT passing `_is_specific_pattern` (bare names) promote ONLY when at least one prod-non-test importer matched via the prefix arm (the imported module is compound — `pattern.X` or `pattern/X`).\n\n### Implementation sketch\n\n```python\ndef _module_match_kind(imported: str, pattern: str) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"Return 'exact' if imported equals pattern; 'prefix' if compound\n    submodule; None if no match.\"\"\"\n    imported_lower = imported.lower()\n    if imported_lower == pattern:\n        return \"exact\"\n    if imported_lower.startswith(pattern + \".\") or imported_lower.startswith(pattern + \"/\"):\n        return \"prefix\"\n    return None\n```\n\nThe promote phase then:\n\n```python\nfor fw in candidates_not_in_existing:\n    patterns = _import_modules_for_framework(fw)\n    for pattern in patterns:\n        is_specific = _is_specific_pattern(pattern)\n        for module_key, importers in module_importers.items():\n            kind = _module_match_kind(module_key, pattern)\n            if kind is None:\n                continue\n            if not is_specific and kind == \"exact\":\n                continue  # bare pattern + bare exact import = ambiguous, skip\n            if any(p and not is_test_file(p) for p in importers):\n                promote(fw)\n                break\n```\n\nThe existing demote phase keeps its semantics unchanged — `_has_prod_import_match` is still consulted with no kind filter for demote (any prod import keeps a framework, whether bare or compound; the demote phase only moves frameworks to dev_frameworks when no prod imports exist at all).\n\n## Closure criterion (per INV-rojip)\n\nThis WI closes `done` when:\n\n1. Bare-name frameworks with compound-submodule prod imports are added to `profile.frameworks` even when the manifest is silent.\n2. The WI-rofiz scenario does NOT regress: a repo with only exact bare `import graphql` (no `graphql.X` submodule imports) does not promote graphql.\n3. INV-rojip's cohorts 2+3 pass:\n   - **bakerydemo** (Django with layered requirements): django framework detected from `from django.db import X` etc., even if requirements.txt is empty/silent.\n   - **chatwoot** (Rails with Gemfile-declared Rails): no regression vs. WI-lohok's autoload exemption; if the Gemfile is silent and no rails imports exist, no promotion fires (different problem; out of scope for this WI).\n4. Hypergumbo self-analysis: no new frameworks falsely promoted on its own codebase.\n5. New tests pin the invariants in `test_profile.py`:\n   - **Promote-bare-with-compound:** `from django.db import X` (compound prefix match) promotes `django`.\n   - **Don't-promote-bare-exact-only:** bare `import graphql` (exact match, no submodule) does NOT promote `graphql`. Pins WI-rofiz lesson.\n   - **Promote-bare-with-slash-submodule:** Ruby `require 'rails/generators'` (slash prefix) promotes `rails`. (NOTE: Ruby usually doesn't import via this path; this test exists for `/`-using ecosystems generally — Go full paths still go through the scoped/compound arm of WI-palol.)\n   - **Mixed-pattern-set:** a framework whose pattern set contains BOTH a specific pattern (`@apollo/server`) AND a bare name (`apollo-server`) promotes via the specific arm of WI-palol regardless of bare-name imports.\n\n## Design tensions to resolve in implementation\n\n1. **Confidence:** should a bare-name promotion carry a `confidence: 0.X` field somewhere so downstream consumers can distinguish manifest-confirmed frameworks from import-promoted-only ones? Currently `RepoProfile.frameworks` is a flat list; this might surface as a separate field or via `dev_frameworks` semantics (which it isn't — dev means dev-only, not low-confidence).\n2. **Threshold on prefix-match volume:** is one compound-submodule import sufficient (current proposal) or should it require ≥ 2 distinct importers? The latter (Mechanism A/D in the follow-on direction explanation) is more conservative but introduces a magic-N.\n3. **Compound-vs-exact match instrumentation cost:** extending `_module_matches` to return match-kind is a small refactor (current API returns `bool`); alternative is a parallel `_module_match_kind` helper. The latter keeps the existing call sites untouched but duplicates the match logic.\n4. **Test/fixture noise:** existing `is_test_file` filter is consulted for both arms. No additional gating needed here.\n\n## Spec / ADR compatibility\n\n- `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` §profile (line 758): unchanged. Detection methodology is not constrained.\n- No ADR governs framework-detection methodology. ADR-0022 (language profile registry) remains deferred and orthogonal.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Chatwoot-style Rails-without-Gemfile** (Rails autoload, no Bundler declaration, no `require` calls anywhere). No import edges → no signal. Separate mechanism class (file-structure heuristic: `app/controllers/`, `config/routes.rb`, `config/application.rb`). File as a separate INV if it surfaces in a real cohort.\n- **Bare-name frameworks with no compound-submodule convention.** Some ecosystems and frameworks legitimately use only bare `import <name>` then access via `<name>.X` attribute on the module object (Python's `import flask; app = flask.Flask(...)` pattern is one example). These won't promote under this WI. If empirical bakeoff shows this is a significant gap, the follow-on is Mechanism A/D — count threshold on bare exact imports.\n- **Per-language whitelist (Mechanism C)** is explicitly NOT this WI's approach. Reintroduces the curation tax WI-palol was designed to escape; reserve as last-resort fallback if Mechanism B fails on bakeoff.\n\n## Bakeoff plan\n\nSingle BROAD cycle on cohort:\n\n- apollo-server/smoke-test (WI-palol cohort 1, must still pass — regression check)\n- bakerydemo (Django layered requirements, INV-rojip cohort 2)\n- chatwoot (Rails Gemfile-declared, INV-rojip cohort 3 — confirms no regression vs. WI-lohok)\n- hypergumbo self-analysis (FP audit, must not promote frameworks the repo doesn't use)\n\nClosure transitions WI-bare-promote → `done` with `awaits_bakeoff_validation` tag. INV-rojip transitions `violated` → `pending_validation` once both WI-palol AND WI-bare-promote bakeoffs land; then `satisfied` after a second BROAD cycle confirms no regression on the validation tree."  # 1e5f
    fields:  # 1e5f
      statement: "A bare-name framework pattern (e.g., 'django', 'flask', 'rails') promotes to profile.frameworks when at least one prod-non-test source file imports a module matching the pattern via the PREFIX arm of _module_matches (compound submodule like 'django.db' or 'rails/generators'); exact bare imports alone do NOT promote, preserving the WI-rofiz lesson on FP-prone bare-name installs."  # 1e5f
